                            'sections' => [
                                ['type' => 'intro', 'title' => 'Right word, wrong partner', 'body' => '"He did a mistake" is understandable and wrong; mistakes are made. At C2, collocation errors are almost the only errors you have left — and they are exactly what makes writing feel foreign. This lesson drills the partnerships that educated prose relies on.'],
                                ['type' => 'explanation', 'title' => 'High-stakes partnerships', 'body' => "Verb + noun:\n- draw a distinction / a conclusion / criticism (NOT \"pull a conclusion\")\n- pose a threat / a question / a challenge\n- meet a deadline / a demand / a standard\n- strike a balance / a deal / a chord\n- bear responsibility / the cost / no resemblance\n\nAdjective + noun:\n- a foregone conclusion (result known in advance)\n- a moot point (no longer worth debating)\n- mitigating circumstances\n- an unmitigated disaster\n\nAdverb + adjective (intensity must match convention):\n- bitterly disappointed, deeply flawed, wildly inaccurate, painfully slow — but not \"bitterly flawed\" or \"deeply inaccurate\".", 'body_id' => 'Kolokasi adalah pasangan kata yang lazim: "make a mistake" (bukan "do"), "draw a conclusion", "pose a threat", "meet a deadline", "strike a balance", "bear responsibility". Juga pasangan penguat: "bitterly disappointed", "deeply flawed", "wildly inaccurate" — penguat tidak bisa ditukar bebas.'],
                                ['type' => 'examples', 'title' => 'Examples', 'meta' => ['items' => [
